"Macaroni","ingredient/macaroni"]]},{"h":"More from American cuisine","links":[["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Barbecue sauce","dish/barbecue-sauce"],["Beer cake","dish/beer-cake"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Blooming onion","dish/blooming-onion"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"],["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Buffalo wing","dish/buffalo-wing"],["Butter cake","dish/butter-cake"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["Caramel apple","dish/caramel-apple"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Cheesesteak","dish/cheesesteak"],["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"],["Chicken nugget","dish/chicken-nugget"],["Chiffon cake","dish/chiffon-cake"],["Chili con carne","dish/chili-con-carne"],["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"],["Chow-chow (food)","dish/chow-chow-food"],["Chowder","dish/chowder"],["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Clam chowder","dish/clam-chowder"],["Clam dip","dish/clam-dip"],["Club sandwich","dish/club-sandwich"],["Cobb salad","dish/cobb-salad"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Cook's Corner","dish/cook-s-corner"],["Crab cake","dish/crab-cake"],["Croffle","dish/croffle"],["Dagwood Bumstead","dish/dagwood-bumstead"],["Dagwood sandwich","dish/dagwood-sandwich"],["Deep-dish pizza","dish/deep-dish-pizza"],["Desperation pie","dish/desperation-pie"],["Dirty rice","dish/dirty-rice"],["Donut hole","dish/donut-hole"],["Egg in the basket","dish/egg-in-the-basket"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/c4/Johnny_Marzetti.jpg/500px-Johnny_Marzetti.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Matt Johnson from Omaha, Nebraska, United States","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johnny_Marzetti.jpg"}},"ingredient/meringue":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/meringue","n":"Meringue","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Meringue is a type of dessert or candy, traditionally made from whipped egg whites and sugar, and occasionally an acidic ingredient such as lemon, vinegar, or cream of tartar. A binding agent such as salt, flour, or gelatin may also be added to the eggs. The key to the formation of a good meringue is the formation of stiff peaks by denaturing the protein ovalbumin (a protein in the egg whites) via mechanical shear."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-meringue"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meringue"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Lady Baltimore cake","dish/lady-baltimore-cake"]]],["Swiss cuisine","cuisine/swiss-cuisine",[["Zuger Kirschtorte","dish/zuger-kirschtorte"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Sponge cake","ingredient/sponge-cake"],["Cherry","ingredient/cherry"],["Common fig","ingredient/common-fig"],["Pecan","ingredient/pecan"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"],["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Butter cream","ingredient/butter-cream"],["Hazelnut","ingredient/hazelnut"],["Kirsch","ingredient/kirsch"],["Kirschwasser","ingredient/kirschwasser"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/19/Meringues_9027.jpg/500px-Meringues_9027.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Mike Lehmann, Mike Switzerland 12:44, 27 January 2007 (UTC)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meringues_9027.jpg"}},"ingredient/zwieback":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/zwieback","n":"Zwieback","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Zwieback is a form of rusk from German cuisine eaten throughout Europe. It is a type of crisp, sweetened bread, made with eggs and baked twice. It originated in East Prussia. According to Fabian Scheidler, Albrecht von Wallenstein invented Zwieback to feed his mercenary army during the Thirty Years' War. Zwieback is commonly used to feed teething babies and as the first solid food for patients with an upset stomach."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-zwieback"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zwieback"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Scottish cuisine","cuisine/scottish-cuisine",[["Lorne sausage","dish/lorne-sausage"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/79/Zwieback-1.jpg/500px-Zwieback-1.jpg","w":500,"h":386,"by":"No machine-readable author provided. Rainer Zenz assumed (based on copyright claims).","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zwieback-1.jpg"}},"ingredient/mi-gomak":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/mi-gomak","n":"Mi Gomak","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Mie gomak is a Batak thick spicy noodle soup dish served in a coconut milk and andaliman-based broth, that is a specialty of Toba Batak region of  North Sumatra, Indonesia. Other than traditional Batak lands surrounding Lake Toba, this dish is also a specialty of the Sibolga and Tapanuli area. Unlike common Indonesian noodles, the type of noodle used in this dish is a thick one called mie lidi, similar to spaghetti pasta, and thus mie gomak is often described as Batak-style spaghetti. Mie gomak is similar to mie Aceh from a neighbouring province."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-mi-gomak"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mie_gomak"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Acehnese cuisine","cuisine/acehnese-cuisine",[["Mie caluk","dish/mie-caluk"]]],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Mie caluk","dish/mie-caluk"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bird's eye chili","ingredient/bird-s-eye-chili"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Cucumber","ingredient/cucumber"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Kencur","ingredient/kencur"],["Krupuk","ingredient/krupuk"],["Noodle","ingredient/noodle"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"],["Seedling","ingredient/seedling"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/2b/Mie_Gomak_2.jpg/500px-Mie_Gomak_2.jpg","w":500,"h":486,"by":"Gunawan Kartapranata","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mie_Gomak_2.jpg"}},"ingredient/tendon-as-food":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/tendon-as-food","n":"Tendon as food","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The tendons of certain animals (particularly beef tendon) are used as an ingredient in some Asian cuisines, including the Filipino, Chinese, Japanese, Korean, Indonesian, Thai, Laotian, Cambodian and Vietnamese. Tendon is tough and fibrous, but becomes soft after a long period of cooking. In some cases it may be boiled for as long as eight hours, while in other dishes it is prepared by deep frying. It contains large amounts of collagen, and after boiling or stewing, it is sometimes described as mimicking the mouthfeel of high-fat cuts of beef despite its low fat content. One author described the taste of deep-fried tendon as being similar to chicharrón (fried pork belly)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-tendon-as-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tendon_as_food"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Mie kocok","dish/mie-kocok"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bakso","ingredient/bakso"],["Bean sprout","ingredient/bean-sprout"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Meatball","ingredient/meatball"],["Mung bean sprout","ingredient/mung-bean-sprout"],["Mustard plant","ingredient/mustard-plant"],["Noodle","ingredient/noodle"],["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"],["Shallot","ingredient/shallot"],["Surimi","ingredient/surimi"]]}]},"dish/pastizz":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/pastizz","n":"Pastizz","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Maltese cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A pastizz is a traditional savoury pastry from Malta. Pastizzi usually have a filling either of ricotta (pastizzi tal-irkotta or pastizzi tal-ħaxu in Maltese) or curried peas (pastizzi tal-piżelli in Maltese). Pastizzi are a popular and well-known traditional Maltese food. It should not be confused with the Italian pastizz, better known as u' pastizz 'rtunnar."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-pastizz"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pastizz"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Maltese cuisine","cuisine/maltese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Filo","ingredient/filo"],["Mushy peas","ingredient/mushy-peas"],["Puff pastry","ingredient/puff-pastry"],["Ricotta","ingredient/ricotta"]]},{"h":"More from Maltese cuisine","links":[["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Imqaret","dish/imqaret"],["Maltese bread","dish/maltese-bread"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/f/fc/Malta_Pastizzi.JPG","w":500,"h":500,"by":"Chattacha","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malta_Pastizzi.JPG"}},"dish/pataniscas":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/pataniscas","n":"Pataniscas","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Portuguese cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A patanisca (isca in Porto) is a typical Portuguese dish. It is usually made by frying shredded bacalhau (patanisca de bacalhau) in a wheat flour, onion and egg purée, condimented with salt, parsley or pepper. Today, different recipes also include other types of fish (such as tuna), seafood or vegetarian alternatives."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-pataniscas"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pataniscas"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Salt cod","ingredient/salt-cod"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Food From Portugal","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"foodfromportugal.com","note":"Portuguese-run recipe site and video channel of traditional Portuguese dishes.","url":"https://www.foodfromportugal.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Portuguese cuisine","links":[["Alheira","dish/alheira"],["Amêijoas à Bulhão Pato","dish/ameijoas-a-bulhao-pato"],["Arroz de marisco","dish/arroz-de-marisco"],["Bacalhau à Brás","dish/bacalhau-a-bras"],["Bacalhau à Gomes de Sá","dish/bacalhau-a-gomes-de-sa"],["Bacalhau à minhota","dish/bacalhau-a-minhota"],["Bacalhau à Narcisa","dish/bacalhau-a-narcisa"],["Bacalhau à Zé do Pipo","dish/bacalhau-a-ze-do-pipo"],["Bacalhau com natas","dish/bacalhau-com-natas"],["Batatas a murro","dish/batatas-a-murro"],["Bolinhos de bacalhau","dish/bolinhos-de-bacalhau"],["Bolo de bolacha","dish/bolo-de-bolacha"],["Canja de galinha","dish/canja-de-galinha"],["Cocido","dish/cocido"],["Embutido","dish/embutido"],["Encharcada","dish/encharcada"],["Fartura","dish/fartura"],["Feijoada","dish/feijoada"],["Fios de ovos","dish/fios-de-ovos"],["Francesinha","dish/francesinha"],["Gazpacho","dish/gazpacho"],["Pão Alentejano","dish/pao-alentejano"],["Pão de Ló","dish/pao-de-lo"],["Polvo à lagareiro","dish/polvo-a-lagareiro"],["Prego (Portuguese sandwich)","dish/prego-portuguese-sandwich"],["Pudim Abade de Priscos","dish/pudim-abade-de-priscos"],["Pudim Molotov","dish/pudim-molotov"],["Sericaia","dish/sericaia"],["Serradura","dish/serradura"],["Tocino de cielo","dish/tocino-de-cielo"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/04/Lisboa_L1190852_%2824603926953%29.jpg/500px-Lisboa_L1190852_%2824603926953%29.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"tak.wing","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lisboa_L1190852_(24603926953).jpg"}},"dish/sarmale":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/sarmale","n":"Sarmale","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Stuffed leaves—more commonly known by its sub-types stuffed grape leaves,  stuffed vine leaves, or stuffed cabbage leaves—are a food made of leaves rolled around a filling of minced meat, grains such as rice, or both. It is occasionally known in the English-speaking world by its Turkish name, sarma, which, as a dish of the Ottoman Empire, is also used in Armenian and some Central and Eastern European languages. Since the Ottoman Empire's dissolution, its popularity persists in Turkish, Romanian, Moldovan, Bulgarian, Serbian, Hungarian, Bosnian, Croatian, Kosovan, Albanian, Montenegrin, Slovenian, Macedonian, Greek, Armenian, Uzbek, Persian, Iraqi, Levantine, Egyptian, Indian and Bengali cuisines."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-sarmale"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sarmale"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Moldovan cuisine","cuisine/moldovan-cuisine"],["Romanian cuisine","cuisine/romanian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","cou